<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sardar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sardars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sardar is a Persian-derived title meaning chief or commander, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> for nobles, military leaders and heads of armed bands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, sardars were noble chieftains who commanded troops, governed provinces and enjoyed considerable autonomy. The title was also used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> chiefs, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> refer to sardars in the period of its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sardar.jpg" alt="Sardar" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ein-oddole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sardar, from Persian sardār, literally commander, chief or leader, is a title of royalty and nobility that was originally used for Muslim princes, noblemen, kings and other aristocrats. It has also been used for the chief of a Muslim tribe, and it serves as a Persian synonym of the Arabic title Sheikh. The term and its cognates have been used across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> (as Serdar), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> (as Sardor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> (as Sirdar).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The title took different forms in each region. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> a member of the royal Mohammadzai clan was a Sardar in the sense of nobleman, and Sardar-i-Salar meant a field marshal or general of the army. King Amanullah founded the Nishan-i-Sardari, the Order of the Leader, in 1923; its recipients took the titles Sardar-i-Ala or Sardar-i-Ali and received grants of land. The order was disbanded in 1929 and later revived by King Muhammad Zahir Shah. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Sardar-i-Bozorg was the title of Hossein Khan Sardar and his brother Hasan Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a>, and Sardar i-Azam was occasionally used for the head of government.</p><p>In Ottoman Turkey Serdar, or Serdar-i-Ekrem, was the title of the commander-in-chief in several campaigns, and it was also a rank in the Bosnia Eyalet. The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> used it as an honorary title below that of Vojvoda; these were not noble titles, since neither country had a nobility. Janko Vukotić, a military leader and former prime minister of Montenegro, was a Serdar. Sirdar was the official title of the British commander-in-chief of the Anglo-Egyptian army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, tribal chiefs, military commanders and wealthy, influential notables were called Sardars, and the title is used by both Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> used it for important political, tribal, military and religious ranks. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> it marks the chief of a tribe, and several tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a>, Poonch, Sudhanoti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a> area use it as a sign of noble status. The Sardars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> are hereditary village chiefs, and under the Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> a Sardari system was introduced in the old city.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> the Sirdar-Bahadur was a governor general or chief minister of a remote province, comparable in rank to a Mughal subahdar or a British viceroy. In the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and the Gorkha Kingdom, Sardars were chiefs of administration and the military in the provinces, and the rulers of Kapurthala State were styled Sardar. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> the word is used to address senior officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-revolutionary-guard-corps">Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sardar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahmad named his son Timur Shah heir over the elder Sulaiman Shah, whom he thought violent and unpopular with the Kandahari Durranis; after his death in 1772 Shah Wali Khan and <b>Sardar</b> Jahan Khan concealed it, carried the body to Kandahar and proclaimed Sulaiman.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was a hierarchy, with the Chhatrapati and his council (the Ashta Pradhan) at the top, followed by <b>sardars</b> (noble commanders), deshmukhs and patils (district and village headmen) and the peasants who worked the land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He had named his eldest son Kharak Singh as heir but had built no institutions capable of managing the transition, and the Lahore Darbar was left to competing factions of Dogra courtiers, Sikh <b>sardars</b> and army commanders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its member states, however, brought with them the Indian State Forces maintained under British supervision, including the Jodhpur <b>Sardar</b> Risala, the Jaipur infantry and the Ganga Risala, the camel corps of Bikaner, which had served in both world wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bakhtiyari leaders joined the Tabriz rebels, and <b>Sardar</b> Assad's forces captured Tehran in 1909.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Buria arose around 1760, when Sikh <b>sardars</b> seized lands between the Sutlej and the Yamuna as Mughal and Durrani authority collapsed in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/buria-state">Buria State</a></li></ul></section>
